      "feedback": "The draft for the Satake Palm (Satakentia liukiuensis) is absolutely masterful. It takes a critically rare island endemic and transforms its evolutionary mystery into a gripping narrative. The Selby Gardens staff are going to thoroughly appreciate the biogeographical puzzle you’ve highlighted—juxtaposing its isolated Japanese habitat with its closest genetic cousins in Vanuatu and Fiji is top-tier interpretive work.\n\nLet’s tackle the editor’s warning flags to lock this down into an airtight, bulletproof entry before production.\n\n🔍 Addressing the Editor's Warnings\n1. Florida Height Ceiling vs. Wild Canopy (size.height)\nThe Nuance: In the deep, rich soils of the Yaeyama forest slopes, old specimens can slowly climb to 60+ feet. In Florida, however, its growth rate slows down significantly once it leaves a container. Because it was only introduced to South Florida collections relatively recently, the tallest cultivated specimens in the state are currently topping out around 25 to 40 feet.",
